<section id="lab">
  <div class="lab-inner">
    <div class="sec-label reveal">What is Reverie Lab</div>
    <p class="lab-text reveal">Reverie Lab is an <strong>independent research umbrella</strong> for work at the intersection of artificial intelligence, biological systems, and compute infrastructure.</p>
    <p class="lab-text reveal">The goal is not to build the next app. It is to work on <strong>the layer beneath</strong> — the infrastructure, tools, and systems that decide what AI can become. Less energy. More capability. Closer to how nature actually computes.</p>
    <div class="pillars reveal">
      <div class="pillar"><div class="n">01</div><div class="name">Compute</div><div class="desc">Software infrastructure that makes next-generation hardware usable by the engineers who already build AI today.</div></div>
      <div class="pillar"><div class="n">02</div><div class="name">Biology</div><div class="desc">How living systems encode intelligence — cellular aging, genetic circuits, and the programming of life itself.</div></div>
      <div class="pillar"><div class="n">03</div><div class="name">Intelligence</div><div class="desc">The architectural and physical limits of intelligence — and what comes after the GPU era.</div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- NEUROBRIDGE -->
<section id="neurobridge" class="feat">
  <canvas id="nb-canvas"></canvas>
  <div class="feat-in">
    <div class="center reveal">
      <div class="badge"><span class="dot"></span>An Idea I Want To Build</div>
      <h2 class="ftitle">NeuroBridge</h2>
      <div class="fsub">Compute infrastructure for neuromorphic AI</div>
    </div>

    <div class="why reveal">
      <div class="kicker">Why I want to build this</div>
      <p>AI runs on hardware that throws most of its energy away as heat. Your brain runs on about <em>20 watts</em> — less than a dim light bulb — and still outthinks every model we have ever built. Brain-inspired chips that close that gap already exist: Intel's Loihi 2, IBM's NorthPole, <strong>25–100× more efficient</strong> per inference. The hardware is here.</p>
      <p>What is missing is the software. There is no layer that lets the millions of engineers who live in PyTorch actually <em>use</em> these chips without abandoning everything they know and learning an entirely new paradigm. So the chips sit in research labs while the rest of the world burns through data-center electricity to run models the old way.</p>
      <p>In 2007, NVIDIA did not win because it had the best silicon. It won because it built <strong>CUDA</strong> — the software layer that made the hardware usable to ordinary developers. That one decision turned a graphics-card company into the most valuable company on earth. Whoever builds CUDA for neuromorphic computing decides what the next era of intelligence runs on.</p>
      <p>I want to build NeuroBridge because nobody else is building that bridge — and because I refuse to accept that the future of intelligence should be bottlenecked by how much electricity a building can buy. The plan: convert a PyTorch model into an event-driven spiking network in <em>one line</em>, benchmark it against the GPU baseline, and deploy it on neuromorphic hardware. No rewrites. No new religion. Just plug in and measure. It would be open source from day one, because a foundational layer should belong to everyone who builds on it.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="stats reveal">
      <div class="stat"><div class="v"><em>25&ndash;100&times;</em></div><div class="t">Energy efficiency of the<br>target hardware vs GPU</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="v">1</div><div class="t">Line of code<br>(the goal)</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="v"><em>Open</em></div><div class="t">Would be open<br>source, MIT</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="grid2 reveal">
      <div class="cell"><div class="l">The Problem</div><div class="h">AI compute is fundamentally broken</div><div class="x">GPU-based inference is <strong>100× more energy-intensive</strong> than biological neural computation. Data-center energy is scaling out of control — and demand keeps climbing.</div></div>
      <div class="cell"><div class="l">The Gap</div><div class="h">The hardware exists. The software doesn't.</div><div class="x">Loihi 2 and NorthPole are <strong>25–100× more efficient</strong> than GPU equivalents. But nothing connects them to mainstream AI. The hardware is ready. The tooling isn't.</div></div>
      <div class="cell"><div class="l">What others tried</div><div class="h">Why it hasn't worked — until now</div><div class="x">Intel's Lava forces new paradigms. IBM's TrueNorth can't run standard models. Academic work stays in papers. <strong>Nobody built the bridge between worlds.</strong></div></div>
      <div class="cell"><div class="l">The Solution</div><div class="h">The CUDA moment for neuromorphic</div><div class="x">The plan: NeuroBridge would convert PyTorch to spiking networks, benchmark energy vs GPU, and deploy on brain-inspired hardware. <strong>No rewrites. Just plug in and measure.</strong></div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="term reveal">
      <div class="bar"><i class="r"></i><i class="y"></i><i class="g"></i><span>neurobridge_concept.py &mdash; sketch</span></div>
<pre><span class="cm"># The interface I want this to have</span>
<span class="cm"># Load any existing PyTorch model — no changes needed</span>
model = torch.<span class="st">load</span>(<span class="st">"my_model.pt"</span>)

<span class="cm"># One-line conversion to neuromorphic</span>
snn = <span class="st">Bridge</span>(model, target=<span class="st">"loihi2"</span>).convert()

<span class="cm"># Then benchmark it honestly against the GPU baseline</span>
results = <span class="st">Benchmark</span>(model, snn).run()</pre>
    </div>

